[[0,1265252,0,3,0,3,"2016-09-02T12:51:24","SilverBanana","",[],"","American troops in Brewster body armor, 1917","http://i.imgtc.com/3aoZIq0.jpg","pics"],[1,6223350,0,2,0,2,"2016-09-02T12:51:28","SilverBanana","",[],"<p><a href=\"http://www.historyisnowmagazine.com/blog/2014/3/5/bizarre-world-war-i-the-body-armor-invention-that-failed#.V8l14Eazzqk=\">http://www.historyisnowmagazine.com/blog/2014/3/5/bizarre-world-war-i-the-body-armor-invention-that-failed#.V8l14Eazzqk=</a></p>"],[1,6224806,0,1,0,1,"2016-09-02T16:17:42","Grospoliner","",[],"<p>For it to stop a battle rifle round it would have had to have been some hefty stuff, bogging the troop down and leaving the wearer vulnerable in close combat.</p>"],[2,6233755,6224806,0,0,0,"2016-09-03T19:20:25","choikwa","",[],"<p>It would've probably stopped submachine gun or pistols... actually I think it would've been perfect for close quarter, but not melee.</p>"],[1,6225799,0,0,0,0,"2016-09-02T18:15:53","ShowMeYourKitties","",[],"<p>Does the Brewster armor have any relation to Caleb Brewster?</p>\n<p>He was a member of the Culprer spy ring during the revolution and became a blacksmith later in life (even though he died 80 years before this photo)</p>"]]
